.env
.env.*
!.env.perps.example
*.key
*.pem
secrets.json
CREDENTIALS_REFERENCE.md

# ---- Python ----
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ---- Logs & runtime data ----
*.log
*.csv
data/
*.sqlite
*.db

# ---- Sniper / digest runtime state (machine-local; Railway seeds its own) ----
sniper_data/
# `reports/*` and NOT `reports/` — git never descends into an excluded
# DIRECTORY, so with the bare form the negation below is dead. Verified: with
# `reports/`, `git check-ignore -v reports/expansion_research_log.md` still
# reports it ignored by that very line.
reports/*
# [2026-09-11] reports/ was ignored as "digest runtime state" and the fleet's
# JUDGEMENT LAYER then moved in behind that line. The research log is the one
# file in there that is a durable record rather than a rendering: ~20
# refutations over 1,762 lines whose entire job is that no idea is tested
# twice — and it lives in ONE un-backed-up copy that no worktree and no fresh
# clone can see. A negation cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded, which is why the line above is `reports/*`, not `reports/`.
# NARROW ON PURPOSE: un-ignoring `reports/*.md` would track 115 files / 1.3 MB
# of daily renderings in one commit. That is Eamon's call, not a side effect of
# this one; the refutation record is the part that must survive a machine.
!reports/expansion_research_log.md

# ---- Backups / editor / OS cruft ----
*.bak
*.~*
.DS_Store
.fuse_hidden*
untitled folder/

# ---- macOS launchd service file (not used on Railway) ----
com.eamon.tri-arb.plist

